WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.026
You already have years of lecture decks.

2
00:00:03.076 --> 00:00:09.691
Turned into video, they can teach on their own: before class, for revision, or for an online cohort.

3
00:00:10.191 --> 00:00:12.091
Don't start with your whole course.

4
00:00:12.141 --> 00:00:16.538
Begin where video helps most, like one assessment explainer.

5
00:00:16.588 --> 00:00:20.591
Every student needs it, and it saves you answering the same emails.

6
00:00:21.091 --> 00:00:25.174
A fifty-minute lecture rarely works as one fifty-minute video.

7
00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:30.481
Give each concept its own short video, so students can find the part they need later.

8
00:00:30.981 --> 00:00:34.588
Swap dot points for what you'd actually say to a student.

9
00:00:34.638 --> 00:00:40.360
Short sentences work better for voices and captions, so check how technical terms are pronounced.

10
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:47.672
Pick a voice and a slightly slower speed for dense material, turn on captions, and watch it all with sound.

11
00:00:47.722 --> 00:00:51.540
Then download the MP4 and add it to your course yourself.

12
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.496
Start with one lecture: one deck and an hour.

13
00:00:55.546 --> 00:01:00.059
On Pro, A.I. directs the video, timing each scene to your narration.

14
00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:03.637
Try one on Basic at powerpoint2vid.com.

